{"name":"Ridgeline","hex":"#7a5d46","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ridgeline","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The topmost edge along a mountain ridge.","The topmost edge along a roof, where its sloping sides meet."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ridgeline"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ridge","blocks":[{"text":"A ridge is a long, narrow, elevated geomorphologic landform, structural feature, or a combination of both separated from the surrounding terrain by steep sides. The sides of a ridge slope away from a narrow top, the crest or ridgecrest, with the terrain dropping down on either side. The crest, if narrow, is also called a ridgeline. Limitations on the dimensions of a ridge are lacking. Its height above the surrounding terrain can vary from less than a meter to hundreds of meters. A ridge can be either depositional, erosional, tectonic, or a combination of these in origin and can consist of either bedrock, loose sediment, lava, or ice depending on its origin. A ridge can occur as either an isolated, independent feature or part of a larger geomorphological and/or structural feature. Frequently, a ridge can be further subdivided into smaller geomorphic or structural elements."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/%E4%B8%AD%E6%A9%AB%E5%85%AC%E8%B7%AF%E7%9C%BA%E6%9C%9B%E6%A2%A8%E5%B1%B1%E5%8F%8A%E9%9B%AA%E5%B1%B1%E5%8D%97%E7%A8%9C2.jpg/330px-%E4%B8%AD%E6%A9%AB%E5%85%AC%E8%B7%AF%E7%9C%BA%E6%9C%9B%E6%A2%A8%E5%B1%B1%E5%8F%8A%E9%9B%AA%E5%B1%B1%E5%8D%97%E7%A8%9C2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ridge","wikidata":"Q740445"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Tsubakurodake_from_Otenshodake_2002-8-22.jpg/500px-Tsubakurodake_from_Otenshodake_2002-8-22.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/c